  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, producing showers and scattered \nthunderstorms. A few storms may be severe with isolated damaging \nwind gusts the primary hazard.\n\nIncreasingly moist isentropic ascent aloft, ahead of the next \nlow pressure system, will allow widespread cloud cover, \ninitially in the mid/upper-levels, to lower and thicken into \nwidespread low-level clouds generally from west to east between \nabout daybreak and midday Wednesday. Periodic and isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms should blossom and \noverspread our region generally from west to east beginning \naround daybreak and continuing through Wednesday afternoon as \nweak to moderate CAPE, including elevated CAPE, is released by \nthe following: the isentropic ascent with a moisture tap to the\nGulf; low-level convergence/moist ascent along surface trough\naxes accompanying shortwave troughs embedded in W'erly\nmid/upper-level flow; downshear outflow boundaries interacting\nfavorably with ambient low-level vertical wind shear amidst\nprimarily moderate to strong deep-layer bulk shear.\n\nThe main area of showers and scattered thunderstorms will arrive \nlate Wednesday afternoon through late evening as a broken band of \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms cross the region. A sharp mid \nlevel trough will cross Ontario and Quebec, with the southern extent \nof height falls spreading east across the Great Lakes and providing \nsome large scale forcing for ascent. The main focus for \nconvection will be a pre-frontal trough that is forecast to \nreach the eastern Great Lakes by early evening. The low pressure \nsystem will capture a narrow plume of deep moisture, with PWAT \nvalues briefly exceeding 2 inches late Wednesday just ahead of \nthe pre-frontal trough.\n\nAny thunderstorms will produce brief downpours, but fast storm \nmotion will likely keep the flood risk low. Strong deep-layer \nbulk shear will overspread the area Wednesday, with boundary \nlayer flow increasing to 40+ knots. Poor mid-level lapse rates \nwill keep instability somewhat limited, but the latest NWP model \nguidance suggests that sufficient instability may develop to \nsupport some severe thunderstorm risk from late afternoon \nthrough mid to late evening. The largely unidirectional wind \nprofiles will support localized damaging wind gusts as the \nprimary severe convective weather hazard.\n\nShowers and scattered thunderstorms will taper off from northwest to \nsoutheast overnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few showers may linger Thursday mainly across the \nSouthern Tier, with dry weather returning Friday through Saturday.\n\nThe cold front will stall across Ohio and PA Thursday as a weak \nbaroclinic wave moves east down the boundary. This wave will produce \nan area of organized rain to our south across Ohio and PA. Most of \nthis will miss our region, although a few showers may develop across \nthe Southern Tier. Otherwise, the rest of the area will be mainly \ndry Thursday with a good deal of cloud cover north of the frontal \nwave. The front will then accelerate southeast and into the Mid \nAtlantic Thursday night as high pressure builds into the central \nGreat Lakes.\n\nDry weather and sunshine will return Friday through Saturday as high \npressure drifts from the Great Lakes east to New England. The next \nchance of showers may arrive by Sunday as the next mid level trough \nand cold front move into the Great Lakes.",
